AN ORDERED LIST DOES NOT INVENT A SECOND STEP.

The same defect as `format-keeps-a-tail-paragraph-inside-its-item`, on an
ordered list. It has its own case for two reasons.

THE HARM IS DIFFERENT IN KIND. A bullet list that gains an item gains a
bullet. An ordered list that gains an item gains a STEP: the document then
describes a two step procedure where the author wrote one step and a note
about it. Prose that explains a step becomes an instruction to carry out, and
a reader following the list does something nobody asked for.

THE INDENT IS DIFFERENT. Content begins three columns after `1. ` and two
after `- `. A fix that hard-coded the bullet width would pass the other case
and fail this one, so this case pins the width to the marker that precedes it.
`BlockFormatter` derives it as indent + marker + one space, which is why both
come out right.
